.TH "rbc::CROSSHAIRS" 3tcl 0\&.5\&.0 "rbc" "Upgraded Tcl/Tk9\&.0-ready RBC package"
.SH NAME
rbc::CROSSHAIRS - Commands in namespace rbc::CROSSHAIRS
.SH "GRAPH CROSSHAIRS CROSSHAIRS COMMAND"
.PP
Commands in this namespace document the graph crosshairs\&.
.PP
CROSSHAIRS is used below as a placeholder for:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
GRAPHINST crosshairs
.fi
.RE
.PP
Crosshairs are a pair of horizontal and vertical lines that intersect at a selected position in the plotting
area\&.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
graph \&.g
\&.g crosshairs configure -position @100,80 -hide no
.fi
.RE
.PP
The vertical line extends across the height of the plotting area and the horizontal line extends across its
width\&.
.PP
The crosshairs are a single graph component\&. There are no named crosshair objects\&.
.SH "CROSSHAIR POSITION"
.PP
The -position option specifies the point where the horizontal and vertical lines intersect\&.
.PP
Positions use widget screen coordinates rather than graph data coordinates and have the form:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
@x,y
.fi
.RE
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
\&.g crosshairs configure -position @120,75
.fi
.RE
.PP
Both x and y are Tk screen distances, so standard Tk distance units may be used:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
\&.g crosshairs configure -position @2c,1c
.fi
.RE
.PP
An empty -position value leaves the crosshairs without a drawable position\&.
.PP
Crosshairs are displayed only when their position lies inside the graph plotting area\&. Therefore making the
crosshairs visible does not cause lines to be drawn if the configured position is outside that area\&.
.PP
Crosshair positions are commonly updated from pointer events:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
bind \&.g <Motion> {
    %W crosshairs configure -position @%x,%y
}
.fi
.RE
.SH "SHOWING AND HIDING CROSSHAIRS"
.PP
The -hide option controls whether crosshairs are enabled:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
\&.g crosshairs configure -hide no
.fi
.RE
.PP
CROSSHAIRS on, CROSSHAIRS off, and CROSSHAIRS toggle provide convenient alternatives:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
\&.g crosshairs on
\&.g crosshairs off
\&.g crosshairs toggle
.fi
.RE
.PP
These operations change the same state represented by -hide\&.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
\&.g crosshairs off
puts [\&.g crosshairs cget -hide]
.fi
.RE
.PP
reports a true value\&.
.PP
Crosshairs are hidden by default\&.
.SH "CROSSHAIR APPEARANCE"
.PP
The crosshair lines may be configured independently of graph elements:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
\&.g crosshairs configure -color red -linewidth 1 -dashes {4 2}
.fi
.RE
.PP
An empty -dashes value selects solid lines\&.
.PP
Crosshairs are drawn directly over the graph and can therefore be moved or removed without requiring the graph
contents to be redrawn\&.
.SH "CROSSHAIRS OPTIONS"
.TS
tab(|) box;
l l.
Option| Description
_
-color color| Specifies the color of the crosshair lines\&. The default is black\&.
-dashes dashList| Specifies the dash pattern used for the crosshair lines\&. An empty value selects solid lines\&. The default is empty\&.
-hide boolean| Specifies whether the crosshairs are hidden\&. The default is yes\&.
-linewidth distance | Specifies the width of the crosshair lines\&. The value must be non-negative\&. The default is 0\&.
-position position| Specifies the intersection of the crosshair lines as a widget screen position of the form @x,y\&. An empty value specifies no drawable position\&. The default is empty\&.
.TE
.PP
Crosshair configuration options may also be supplied through Tk's option database\&. The resource name is
crosshairs and the resource class is Crosshairs\&.
.PP
For example:
.PP
.RS 4n
.nf
option add *Graph\&.crosshairs\&.Color red
option add *Graph\&.Crosshairs\&.Linewidth 1
.fi
.RE

